<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/21st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abraham Accords</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> Accords are agreements, brokered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, by which Israel established diplomatic relations with Arab states, beginning with the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, which signed at the White House on 15 September 2020. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> followed later in 2020. They were the first normalisation agreements between Israel and Arab countries since the peace treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1979 and Jordan in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham-accords.jpg" alt="Abraham Accords" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The White House from Washington, DC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_and_The_First_Lady_Participate_in_an_Abraham_Accords_Signing_Ceremony_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Abraham Accords were preceded by earlier diplomatic milestones between Israel and members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a>: peace treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1979 and Jordan in 1994, and relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> in 1995. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo-accords">Oslo Accords</a> of 1993 and 1995 advanced the Israeli–Palestinian peace process, which later collapsed. The Arab Peace Initiative of 2002 offered normalisation of relations between the Arab world and Israel in return for a full Israeli withdrawal from the occupied territories, a settlement of the refugee question and a Palestinian state with East Jerusalem as its capital.</p><p>In the 2010s Israel and Sunni Arab states drew closer through shared concern about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and by 2016 summits and contacts between officials and security services were openly discussed in major Arab media. In October 2018 the Omani foreign minister visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. In January 2020 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> published a peace plan that foresaw Israeli sovereignty over parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>When Netanyahu's cabinet considered annexing parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> in mid-2020, the Emirati ambassador to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, Yousef Al Otaiba, warned in a newspaper article that it would end normalisation with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> and other Arab states. At the end of June he told the American officials Jared Kushner and Avi Berkowitz that the United Arab Emirates would agree to normalisation if Israel declared annexation off the table. The agreement was announced on 13 August 2020, and annexation was postponed. Within hours Bahraini officials told Kushner and Berkowitz that they wanted to be next, and that deal was closed on 11 September 2020.</p><p>Netanyahu and the foreign ministers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, Abdullah bin Zayed Al Nahyan and Abdullatif bin Rashid Al Zayani, signed the agreements on 15 September 2020 on the Truman Balcony of the White House, in a ceremony mediated by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donald-trump">Donald Trump</a>. The name was chosen to reflect the shared heritage of the Abrahamic religions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> agreed to normalise ties on 23 October 2020. As part of that agreement the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> removed Sudan from its list of state sponsors of terrorism and gave it a loan of 1.2 billion US dollars, and Sudan agreed to pay 335 million dollars in compensation to American victims of terror. On 10 December 2020 Trump announced an agreement between Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, which came with American recognition of Moroccan sovereignty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>. Governments in the Arab world expressed support, while public opinion in many countries remained opposed, particularly because the Accords made no progress on the Israeli–Palestinian conflict.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abraham Accords” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 2010s it pursued an assertive foreign policy, opposing the Muslim Brotherhood, backing allied forces in Libya and Yemen, joining the boycott of Qatar from 2017 to 2021 and signing the <b>Abraham Accords</b> with Israel in 2020.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
